    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21831,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21830,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Memories of the anti-Iraq protests flood through your mind and you cross into the no lobby. The motion is defeated with 30 Tory MPs joining your rebellion, Kennedy’s legacy is intact but you’ve made yourself an easy target. Enjoy running the media circuit explaining your decision for the next few weeks…"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21833,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21832,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "In between speaking to campaign groups about a local fundraiser, 3 notifications reach your phone, the first, BBC News, the motion was defeated by a narrow margin of Tory and Lib Dem rebels. The second, 2 missed calls from Downing Street. You put your phone on silent for the rest of the day."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21835,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21834,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "14 Lib Dems rebel with 9 not voting, the motion is defeated. Privately, it seems as if you’ll take the blame for the failure, but at least no can say you didn’t try."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21837,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21836,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "After hours of deliberations, arguments and maybe screaming, a solution is reached. The party will vote for the motion, on the condition that British bases will not host US missiles, despite Tory dissent, the motion passes, and you come out of the day relatively unscathed, the same can’t be said for Cameron."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21838,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21823,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You obtain a meeting with Osborne, but it’s all for naught. Osborne does privately agree that it’s going too far and too fast, but he can’t slow the inevitable conclusion. Hammond announces that there will be a referendum at some point in the near-future. You head back to headquarters to prepare for the coming referendum, and the coalitions that will be formed…"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21839,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21824,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "All hell breaks loose. The Tories are emboldened to stick with the referendum, with Labour defending your choice. Either way, the coalition is blown to smithereens and we’re headed for an early election. Snap polling already shows a precipitous loss of support in non-university seats, threatening a solid majority of your seats. Buckle down, Nick, you’re in the muddy."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21840,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21825,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You’re able to get Cameron in the room with you and the Lib Dem higher-ups, but in the end, it comes to nothing. Cameron expresses his frustration, but says if he doesn’t grant it, the right-wing of the Tories will revolt against him. Frustrated, you and the higher-ups prep for a referendum. Parties will be fuzzy, and you’ll have to get good at playing everyone off each other."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21842,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21841,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You’re the first Liberal to occupy a significant role in government since Lloyd George nearly a century ago. Whether you’re in the same league as him is now up the voters. Godspeed Nick."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21844,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21843,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Manchester, Cardiff, Sussex, in essence, a grand tour of the country’s intellectual elite. The more time you spend on campus, it seems your main competitor this time round is Natalie Bennett’s Greens, so it’s really a battle of whose can “out-change” each other."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21846,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21845,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You make speeches at more business conferences than you can count, touting the benefits your party has provided to markets and industries. It’s almost a comfortable cycle you follow. Maybe if this election is that bad for you, the business world could be your next home…"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21848,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21847,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Recognising that you may be slightly out of your comfort zone with this, you let Tim Farron take the lead on this campaign. You focus mostly on donor events and fundraisers, but every day you miss the campaign trail. Ah to go back to the halcyon days of 2010…"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21850,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21849,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Farron in the southwest, Davey in the city, Alexander and Kennedy in Scotland, all the across the country, your MPs are getting your message out there, slightly conflicting in some cases but it’s out there nonetheless. There’s always the concern that you’re spreading yourself a little too thin, but as long as your MPs keep strongly supporting the campaign, you should be okay."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21854,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21851,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "This pitch works for the London high-society who are well-informed on the goings of the British government. But for the rest of the country, this pitch falls flat. They’ve seen the chaos, the turmoil, the fact that there will be a Brexit referendum and figure that taking a chance on a weaker Labour candidate will be better for their pocketbooks than a toothless Lib Dem. Resigned, you go to campaign at uni campuses to try and stave off an inevitable defeat"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21855,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21852,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Everywhere you go, a pernicious dread hangs in the breath of the rank-and-file you thought were home base. Their fists wrangle into a rage at the notion of a “changed” Britain. Budget cuts, a welfare slash on Threadneedle Street and No. 10, and a system shattered after the greed of bankers. In their eyes, you’re no better than them, you only facilitate them – as you ride around in ministerial cars."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21856,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21853,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "For the uni voters that you gained in 2010, this is music to their ears. But to the wider LD section that elected MPs in rural territory, this is a harder sell. Traditional values take a while to change, and your rural MPs report tough sledding as Tory and UKIP candidates gain ground. You schedule events with Farron to try and cut into the deficit, but there’s a nagging voice that says this: maybe it won’t be enough."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21875,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21692,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "The optimistic message of the party falls flat under an absolute barrage of negativity from Farage. Miliband soaks up most of the headlines by rebutting Farage, and thus, it’s him who gets the Monday morning headlines. Cameron flies under the radar, while you’re made out to look like the naive fool: much like your 2010 voters."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21876,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21860,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Over and over you try to tie Farage to Cameron with moderate success. After about the 10th time of trying, Miliband chimes in with a request for you to talk about your own policies instead of theirs to laughter throughout the hall. While politicos recognize you did better than expected, the general public doesn’t. All in all though, not a disaster."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21877,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21861,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "As far as strategies go, this is probably the most timid one you could’ve gone with. No one really thought the LDs played a massive role in government, so while you come off as the policy experts, you haven’t expanded your base much. That’s fine if you like the state of the race, but something has to give eventually."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21881,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21691,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You steer the responses towards the benefits of the EU and how it’ll mean more money for the average consumer. Very few questions come along about foreign policy, but you’re able to at least adequately placate the concerns the average Briton has had about the coalition for the first five years."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21882,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21878,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "It has gotten more progressive, but not that much. Your uni supporters will back you all the way, but the average family wants to know about your plans vis a vis the EU and the economy. You perform well, but there was that gear you never really hit."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21883,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21879,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Problem is Nick, people don’t think the economy is getting better. All they see is negative headlines about austerity and assume you’re in on the game. You can’t play the waiting game anymore, and as such, you don’t do great on Question Time. "
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21884,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21880,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Young voters carried you in 2010, but is the environment really the most pressing issue right now? Most voters, even younger ones, are worried about the rise of Brexit or the economy more broadly. Honing in on the environment just doesn’t do it. You get some nice social media posts in uni towns, but overall it’s a disappointing performance."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21887,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21690,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You put out a harsh statement denouncing Cameron for even considering the idea of partnering with UKIP and DUP. The ‘twins of right-wing ethnocentrism’ line really does stick with public, and you see a late uptick of support in key constituencies."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21888,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21885,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "Unfortunately, Miliband and Sturgeon decline the joint press conference citing campaign obligations. You do elicit promises of media communication and similar statements, which do make waves among mainstream media outlets."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21889,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21886,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "The website proves to be a major hit, especially among younger voters. Spoofing the policies only makes UKIP look out of touch with the average Briton, and Cameron is forced to answer some questions about the possibility of the coalition over the following days."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21893,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21689,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You go all around London. College campuses, high-society hangouts, and many other places. You rally with MPs and the average voter, but you’re just not able to convince many. Will you have enough to hold what you have?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21894,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21890,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You speak to a collected assembly of trade unionists who sit contentedly to hear you out. Once you finish your speech, to polite applause, you wade through the crowd, assuring them that Brexit will be stopped in its tracks with their help. But do you even believe your own words?"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 21895,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 21891,
            "candidate": 201,
            "answer_feedback": "You head up to Scotland, a place that you haven’t been since the independence referendum. You’re not in your normal element, and it’s fairly obvious to Scottish voters. Your MPs help somewhat with the confusion, but it’s not a massive success. As you head back to London, you feel a pit in your stomach."
        }
    },
    {
